A||||53||newfie||Fiero or Corvette??||||5||
Z||000000||newfie||12-20-2001||06:42 PM||||I've been a fan of fiero's for a long time and planned on purchasing one in the new year.  The plan was a GT fastback and doing an engine swap, 3.4DOHC.  But recently I started looking at the prices or Corvettes and realized with the money I spend on a fiero I could get a decent C4 corvette.  I never thought I could affard a 'vette and am doing some serious considering.  What should I do?  How do they both compare, pros and cons of each?||||reg||5
Z||000001||mrfiero||12-20-2001||06:53 PM||||You can thank the C5 Vette for the cheap prices of C4's.  There are certainly some good bargains out there on fairly low mileage C4's......I personally would stick with '93-'96 models, but that's just my preference.  <P>As far as how they compare to Fieros?  That's not a fair comparison.....both bring different things to the table.  The Vette is by far faster, but more expensive to drive & maintain.  The Fiero, in my opinion, is a better balanced car overall, but lacks any "punch" performance-wise (in stock trim).  I also think the Fiero looks better.<P>There are also more Vettes out there than Fieros (nice ones, anyways), so I would take the Fiero over a C4 Vette anyday.<P>Now if we were talking C5 Vettes I would say go for the Vette.  They are leap years ahead of the C4 on looks and performance and the Fiero just can't compete with it.<P>I love my '88 Fiero GT's, but if I could afford a C5 Vette I would have sold them a long time ago!  <P> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||VISCERAL||12-20-2001||07:01 PM||||I would really hate to admit it, but the fiero is harder to fix and is getting harder to find obselete parts. Go for the vette, if you can live with that looong hood. Sorry fellow fiero fans. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_1.jpg"> <BR>Patrick Szewczyk<BR>1987 Fiero GT, 5 spd, sunroof CDF clutch, Koni's all around, Ground Control coilovers in the rear, lowered 2" -dropped spindles-front, poly bushings: control arms-sway bars-cradle-motor-dogbone, rear sway bar, pioneer cd player, 180 stat, 176-166 fan switch, MSD Coil, Rodney Dickman's short shifter, bushings, etc..., 11.25" big brakes w/Ebrake, k&n filter, I have a 3800SC SII that's goin in soon, + 17x7.5 MiM Venezias wheels +  225/45/17 and 235/40/17's ---------------Also have a 1996 3800 Series II 25k MOTOR FOR SALE.||||reg||1
Z||000003||wkayl||12-20-2001||08:31 PM||||I "told" my wife I'm going to get a low milage red 93-96 glass top Vette in 4 years (that is when my truck will be paid for).  She said "we will see."<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_2.jpg">    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/usa1017[1].gif">   <BR>Dnyee's 88 Formula, Casey's 86GT, Wayne's 86GT.  <BR>We consider my wife as being "Fiero Challenged"||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||12-20-2001||08:32 PM||||Ive got a 86SE with a Ferrari kit, moded engine with turbo. Recently got a nice C4. The vettes nice for around town driving cause its automatic. The Ferraro is way more fun, more comfortable and will eat the vette alive except off the line. Insurance for you with the vette will prob be higher than the payments. I have a friend whos 6'3 and likes the Ferraro, but hates the vette for him to fit in anyway.<P>------------------<BR>           <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/rogergarrison1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Alex4mula||12-21-2001||10:16 AM||||Fiero is more ergonomic than the C4 Vette and of course more beautiful. With little money you can make it faster than the Vette too. Vettes are common. Fieros are not. Vettes has a huge aftermarket/restoration resources. Fieros is limited but growing. Also I think Fieros are more reliable and has less complicated parts. It's your call.<P>------------------<BR>    Alex4mula<BR>:) Red-Original Owner & White-1/94 with PM (:||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||12-21-2001||12:07 PM||||For god's sake get the Vette. There is no comparision in any area. Other than your personal styling preferences, the Vette is a better car in every way.<P>I prefer the 90-91 since engine-wise, they are speed density but without Opti-Crap, er, Spark. By then most of the good changes to the chassis and suspension had occurred.<P>Problem is, at 6'4", I don't fit in a C4.<P>TK||||reg||1
Z||000007||falconhulk||12-21-2001||03:04 PM||||Terry, trust me you can fit in a C4.  I am 6' 4" and can do it.  Your left leg has to learn to get comfortable in a wierd postion but its definitel doable.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Fformula88||12-21-2001||03:40 PM||||I absolutely love my Fiero without question, but if your wondering whether to get a Fiero or a Vette... I don't even see a comparison.  Corvette all the way.<P>Appearance is subjective, but I always thought Corvette's were great looking cars.  Performance wise its no match.  If you put the money into a Vette that you would in upgrading a Fiero to compete with it, you'd have a killer!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Philphine||12-21-2001||09:43 PM||||if you were thinking of swapping engines anyway you could probaly get both. the vette will be a performer right off, and if you're wanting to change engines in the fiero you could probaly get a nice reliable 4cyl. for not too much cash and do the swap sometime in the future. in the meantime it could be the gas saver/runabout. guess that would depend on what other cars you have or what room you have for cars.||||reg||1
Z||000010||terryk||12-22-2001||01:16 AM||||Trust me, I don't. I've sat in at least 30 different C4's. I don't fit and therefore I don't own one. I have a longer torso and shorter legs (32"). If I sit up in a coupe my head hits. My legs aren't much of a problem except for the annoying tunnel. I could live with that. Now a C5 is fine. I just clear the roof. Maybe another 2 years when they come down a bit more. For now, I'll stick with the Fiero and TR.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 12-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Oreif||12-22-2001||01:48 AM||||Well Corvette's are nice, But I'll stick with my Fiero. It has nothing to do with anything other than personal preference. I always say go with what you want. If a Corvette is in your price range and you find one you like, Buy it. Who knows, you might find a great deal on one and still get a Fiero. Then you will own two of the four 2-seaters GM built.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||BlackStang95||12-22-2001||02:04 AM||||"Fiero is more ergonomic than the C4 Vette and of course more beautiful. With little money you can make it faster than the Vette too. Vettes are common. Fieros are not. Vettes has a huge aftermarket/restoration resources. Fieros is limited but growing. Also I think Fieros are more reliable and has less complicated parts. It's your call"<P>Um, I think everything you said here besides the aftermarket line was  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000013||rogergarrison||12-22-2001||07:07 AM||||stangs at it again (sigh) I owning both and working on both myself fully agree with all those statements except the very last. So far at least, doing work on the Vette has been much simpler (but not cheaper) than the Fiero. I changed a bad alternator completely standing up in less than 5 mins. Changed 8 spark plugs in 15 mins, filled radiator without a technical degree. Im 6' and with seat all the way back, I still feel a little cramped...........but not so in the Ferraro.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Daleford||12-22-2001||08:01 AM||||Stand back, look at both cars, you will buy the Vette.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||BlackStang95||12-22-2001||12:06 PM||||Well, more beautiful could be subjective.  I'm confident if you polled people it'd be overwhelmingly in the vettes favor though.  Vettes common???!  Maybe if you live in beverly hills.  They turn heads everywhere here, I've yet to see a z06 or C anything on the road.  Enough said.  ||||reg||1
Z||000016||SKIDMARK||12-22-2001||12:45 PM||||My plan is to end up with one of each. You can't compare apples to oranges but you can put them in the same bowl. My garage would look a lot better with a vette next to my Fiero.<P>------------------<BR>White '85 4cyl coupe<BR>Black '86 v-6 SE<BR>'81 CB900F Super Sport||||reg||1
Z||000017||Predator||12-22-2001||01:04 PM||||The Fiero can be nice with a lot less money for insurance etc. The Corvette is best overall if you have the money. Personally I would take a stock Corvette over a stock Fiero any day. Your talking a 90's Corvette to a 80's Fiero. There is no contest Corvette wins hands down. Don't get me wrong I like my Fiero, but if I had the money I would own a Corvette. It all comes down to personal preference.||||reg||1
Z||000018||terryk||12-22-2001||01:26 PM||||I wasn't considering money. Vette's are more expensive to own. Absolutely.<P>I don't know about a Fiero being faster than a C4/C5. Take two stock cars, V6 Fiero and V8 Vette. The Fiero loses. Put any amount of money into the Fiero and that same amount into the Vette, the Fiero loses.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000019||Hairy_Fiero||12-22-2001||02:30 PM||||Okay how bout this. Buy a brand new Vette out the door around 50 grand? Take an 88 Fiero GT for 5 grand. Now put 45 grand into the Fiero Gt  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif"> to make the cars equal in price. The Fiero would be a big time contender. You can't compare the two until you make the price tag equal. The Fiero and especially it's owners get the shaft from everyone. Keep the Fiero in it's price range. Compare it to other cars of the same generation and price. This is why the Fiero is so attractive to some people.  ||||reg||13
Z||000020||rogergarrison||12-22-2001||03:50 PM||||well, dont know about where you are, but c4s/c5s are almost as plentiful around here as tauruses. And no Reynoldsburg is not an affluent neighborhood.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Formula||12-22-2001||05:03 PM||||corvettes are more common then nice fieros.<P>if youve never seen a "C whatever" corvette then youve never seen a corvette.  they are all either C1 C2 C3 C4 or C5.<P>never seen a ZO6?  Man, those are a dime a dozen anymore... go visit a middle class town sometime. ||||reg||1
Z||000022||terryk||12-22-2001||05:42 PM||||Agreed. I like the Fiero because it is unique. Definitely something different. I'm just taking the stand that a Fiero at any extreme is not a C4 or C5.<P>Even if I bought a Vette, I'd still have the Fiero. I hated them in the 80's. They just grow on you.||||reg||14
Z||000023||XDrewX||12-23-2001||12:36 AM||||What years were the C4 ?<P>Let me tell everyone what I call my FIERO....<P>" The poor man's Corvette "<P>This annotation arrived when ALOT of my friends and associates started buying Vette's.<P>Please dont flame me, I LOVE my Fiero, But if I could afford a 2000 or newer Vette, I would have it.<P>I love the ones where the whole frontend tilts forward, that is the only one I would own..... it seems like it would be so easy to work on the engine.<P>Peace<P>DAP<P>||||reg||1
Z||000024||Smoooooth GT||12-23-2001||01:23 AM||||Here you a mr2-vette...lol  cool though... <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mr2-vettte.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smooooothgtsig_9.gif">                       <BR>      <P><A HREF="http://www.thefibersmith.com" TARGET=_blank>www.thefibersmith.com</A>   <BR>Technical  Support (903) 825-7744 Steve<BR>"Smoooooth GT"<BR>Shaved door handles, sidetrim, exhaust outlet's, etc.... smooooth!!!||||reg||1
Z||000025||BlackStang95||12-23-2001||02:26 AM||||"Okay how bout this. Buy a brand new Vette out the door around 50 grand? Take an 88 Fiero GT for 5 grand. Now put 45 grand into the Fiero Gt  to make the cars equal in price."<P><BR>Im really tired of seeing this argument.  Of course modding an old car is gonna be cheaper than buying a new sports car.  If you want to go that route..why stop at a fiero gt?  Buy a rotting old camaro, mustang, anything for $300 and put $49,700 into it to make the price equal.  Or even $4700 to make its price even with the fiero gt, it'll smoke it.  The reason people buy fast factory cars is ease of parts, no stress, warranty, no need to worry about putting a 5.7 liter into a car designed for a 2.8 liter, etc.  Some people enjoy souping up cars, some enjoy driving fast stock ones, and some like myself fall somewhere in the middle.  It's all up to the individual.||||reg||1
Z||000026||rogergarrison||12-23-2001||04:52 AM||||It is ez, you can even sit on the front tire to work. Alignment is soooooo ez, just the old shim style.Loosen a nut and drop in or take out a shim till it steers the way you want. The different 'C' types refer to body style changes. The newer one I think started with 97 model?, C5. The C4 started with the 84 model till the C5 came out. ||||reg||1
Z||000027||blue byu||12-23-2001||07:14 AM||||The 3.4 swap won't give a fair comparison.  Now a Northstar fiero, now that's a fair comparison.  I haven't been on the forum for a while, but since the last time, Chris Moore and I have been doing the conversion to my car.  In my case, I thought through the same question.  I got my '87 GT with the same thought in mind - eventually upgrade suspension and motor (this all started 6 years ago).  I rationalized the decision by the fact I now have a car that will run very close to a c5 vette in the quarter, same on the skid pad, but I have less than 1/2 the money in it, and it's a much more unique car.  I'm not saying it would compete head to head with a c5, but Chris's northstar's run identical time's with the c5 in the quarter.  Good enough for me!  If you can do something like that, I'd do the fiero everyday of the week.||||reg||1
Z||000028||Philphine||12-23-2001||07:26 AM||||i would'nt want to put 45k into my fiero. the low cost is part of the appeal. right now if i took my least expensive fiero (cost $125), was able to do a $1000 (maybe even less) 4.9 swap, spare wheels, same color macco repaint, spare stereo stuff, etc., i've got a 2-3k fun car that would have as a main pride point the low cost. not even half of what i paid for a decent c3 vette. plug the numbers into the fiero i paid the most for (2k) and it's still a cheap car (consider some of the things you'd refurb on the cheaper car are done on the 2k one).<P>i know i'm talking rock bottom but you get the idea.||||reg||1
Z||000029||Slammed Fiero||12-23-2001||12:18 PM||||How many people on this forum have actually spent some time in a c4 vette Besides Roger and I? Some of your assumptions and ideas are wayyyy off.<P>Yeah , I can shove a big motor in my Fiero and make it faster than any vette. But with the C4 your starting that much further ahead. You can put about 1/2 the work into a c4's suspension and get it to outhandle the best of the Fiero's. They just start on a higher level than a fiero.<P>As for reliability? The only things that will ever really need to be replaced are alternators (for some reason they eat them) 2 every 4 years of regular driving. And for some reason the gas pedal is weak and the pedal itself it will brake. Add a Master cylinder into that and a set of mufflers and that is all we have had to replace on ours Since 1987.<P>They are 2 different Cars. A lot of Fiero people get up in arms when the corvette is mentioned.  People come up with these strange ideas even though you have never spent much time in one.<P>The build quality of the cars , wear wise is kinda lacking. Not in a fit in finish sense , but the Vette creaks far more than My Fiero , and my Fiero has far stiffer suspension and more miles.<P>There both great cars for different reasons, make the decision that you think best suits you. Spend  some time with both cars. The best C4 to get is the 96 Collectors edition with the LT4 (6spd car) has Bigger brakes and the 330hp LT4. You can pick up a good L98 Car cheap , probably the best performance bang for the buck there is going. 155mph stock , 5.7 sec to sixty. Great platform for performance. Aim to get a 86.5 and later car, mid 86 they added aluminum heads to the cars , upping the HP , 87 they added a different cam profile , and 88 they changed the rear suspension.<P>Get What you want , don't believe much of what you read on subjects like this. There is obviously going to be a lot of biased. Both cars have their merits. <P>IF you want to build an all out performance car / world killer, get the Vette you won't be as frustrated as trying to build a fast Fiero. If you want a Fun , uniquie car with lots of performance potential , get the Fiero.<P>The point is , get what you want.<P>JM <BR>Sometimes driver of a nice 87 Z51 , Koni's , Borla Exhaust , BBK TB , Guldstrand suspension <P>------------------<BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals, Big Bore TB, 70Hp Shot of NOS Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
Z||000030||BlackStang95||12-23-2001||06:57 PM||||For the first time ever I agree with jm.||||reg||1
Z||000031||Joe Torma||12-23-2001||07:51 PM||||I read a Vette owner's quote in a magazine, kinda an ineresting statement:<P>Vette owners don't actually own their cars, they just preserve them for future generations<P>To hell with preserving...I'm modifying the cr@p out of my car.  But if I could afford a Vette, it would be a new convertible and a stereo would all I would have to do to it to make me happy.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>But I kinda like being the "underdog" that beats everyone at their own game.<P>------------------<BR>Its never going to be finished...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig2_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000032||artherd||12-24-2001||04:11 AM||||They are two VERY different (and yet oddly similar in a way) cars.<P>I've driven two.  One was an 87 (I belive, may have infact been older) with a 4-speed, man that thing was a dog, and rattled like nobody's buisness. HORRIBLE car. lame digital dash, build quality right out of the convertible f-body leauge...<P>I've also driven a (much more cared-for) ZR-1.  Now that thing is a bit of an exotic.  405bhp 32-valve (yes, a real DOHC motor) 6-speed.  great car.  Expensive car. Still a vette, and still a C4. But a nice example thereof.  The C5 that should have been.  Powerplant at least. the LS1 is laughable. (actually, it's a marvel, that it could be done at all) but I digress.<P><BR>The Fiero is an entirely different proposition, it is a $40k Italian mid-engine sports car living in disguise as a $2k american POS hobo.<P>What do you want?  What makes your blood *really* boil?   <P>If you could have any car, price no object, what would it be?  How do you get as close to that as you possibly can while still making the mortgauge payment every month?<P>WHAT WILL PUT THE BIGGEST GRIN ON YOUR FACE?<P>For me, the answer is my Northstar Fiero.<P>For you... ?<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000033||BlackStang95||12-24-2001||01:11 PM||||Dude, the fiero has more in common with the cavalier than a ferrari.  I like them too but let's not kid ourselves.  It's a 2-4000 dollar sports coupe that some guys with complexes think is a 40k italian exotic.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000034||terryk||12-24-2001||08:08 PM||||"WHAT WILL PUT THE BIGGEST GRIN ON YOUR FACE?"<P>Turbo Regal/Grand National. My Supercharged Ciera GT is fun too, but the TR is just criminal.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000035||rogergarrison||12-24-2001||08:48 PM||||I remember when the FBI bought 200 of those to use as undercover cars. 'Sleepers'||||reg||1
Z||000036||FieroKyle||12-25-2001||12:35 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by terryk:<BR><B>"WHAT WILL PUT THE BIGGEST GRIN ON YOUR FACE?"<P>Turbo Regal/Grand National. My Supercharged Ciera GT is fun too, but the TR is just criminal.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have an 85 Regal N/A. That car is lots of fun. Slower than **** , but fun. I can't imagine that car with a turbo making 13 sec 1/4s. The great part is the handling. Someways I like a terrible handling car because its funner to drive. Bouncing all over the road with the back-end half sideways roaring like a bat out of hell. Sure the Fiero or Corvette or any sports car can handle with ease not close to the edge. Its fun though, anyways off the topic.<P>Corvettes are common, but I am sorry gonna have to take one over that Fiero. Its fast out of box and really for fun driving and dragging and cornering, the car is fine stock. Don't have to do anything to it, its just fast. The Fiero needs little suspension and a v-8 swap to even compete with the Vette. I would take a Vette over a Fiero, a v-8 Fiero thats nicely done over a Vette.<P>45k into a Fiero? Is that POSSIBLE???!!! lol, just for dream Fiero I think I came to 26k for everything, I mean everything I would like done to it. I think that includes a rework of the suspension, IMSA kit, and an Archie special with many other little upgrades.<P>How can you compare even? Hell I hate Mustangs, but I drove my friends 300 hp 5.0 and I grinning the whole. I still don't like them, I wouldn't buy one (well may be a mint 5.0L Black from the late eighties thats got around 300 to 400 hp if I could afford it), but its was a fun 5 miles.<P>"poor man's Corvette"<BR>exactly<P>The upgrade for the Fiero is the T/A or Corvette, take your pick, not a 3.4 swap. Its not even worth the money i think.<P>If you can afford high insurance, hi cost maintence, hi cost parts, just go with a Vette.<P>But are you going for a fun car, a Vette, or a Vette Killer?<P>nuff said, and I agree with most of the above.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000037||FieroRacer||12-25-2001||12:42 AM||||If you want to do a fair comparison.  Imagine if the Fiero had been continued to develop as they planned.  Put 23K 2002 Fiero GT with FWD LS1 Setup and a 6 speed manual up against a 50K Z06 and see who wins?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000038||terryk||12-25-2001||01:22 AM||||The Z06.||||reg||1
Z||000039||Dumba$$||12-25-2001||01:32 AM||||dude, you cant buy a vette, cuz guy's in vetts never get on the gas  <P>personally , if i spent 35-55ish grand on a vett id probibly never get on the gas in fear that id get my ass kicked by a 400 hp civic or somthing along the lines of that <P><BR>think of it this way <BR>dont buy a vette and sepend the same amt. of money on a fiero <BR>just think of what even 15- 20 grand could do to a fiero <BR>a hell of alot more than 15-20 could doto a vette<P>i know if i had that kind of $$ id have one hell of a fiero  and if that still wasent fast enough, id go buy a crotch rocket <BR>then id have alot of wet dreams  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>butt keep in mind that no matter how nice, or how fast your car is, <BR>there is always somthing nicer or faster  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>if youve got 50 grand for a new vette  why not pull another 30 grand out of your ass and get a viper  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>Rich<BR>Sioux Falls SD<BR>Red 84 SE<BR>Gold 86 SE <BR>98 acura cl 3.0<P>ever notice that people that own a fiero, either have, or have had other fieros?||||reg||1
Z||000040||Slammed Fiero||12-25-2001||01:41 AM||||Yeah and GM is going to build a 23 grand car that beats their flagship Corvette... ??<P>it's called shooting yourself in the foot. In GM's eyes nothing will be faster than the vette PERIOD... I have read many articles that say there is a reason that the new z06 has advertised Hp of 405hp.. Remember the car in the 90's they called "King of the hill" The all mighty ZR1? from 93 until it's demise it put out 405hp.. Hmmm.... The car wasn't all that great. From a  drivers standpoint it belonged on the autobahn , not the track. Too heavy. If you put the 2 on a track or an autocross course the C4 was faster, bar none. You could feel the weight difference between the two cars , in every aspect of driving.<P>Still would love to have a ZR1 Though.<P>As for this Vette Vs Fiero thread. Put what ever motor you want into your fiero , But for gods sake don't do it to try to beat vettes and Porsche's. Do it because you want to personalize the car. Smallblock , northstar , held Motorsports suspension..yadda yadda. Those cars began life with a silver spoon in their mouths, they had a higher degree of engineering put into them , they have a larger aftermarket for themselves which only makes them faster and faster. You will learn a hard lesson if you dump dollar after dollar into a Fiero in an attempt to beat the other guys high dollar exotic, you Can't. You can beat a lot of them no doubt. But when it comes down to it , no matter what you bolt on or drop in the Vettes , 911's and others out there that have been modified 1/2 as much as a fiero , will be twice as fast. (not talking 1/4 mile)<BR>I think some people forget that for every 1 person who modifies a Fiero , there are quite a few more that modify their vette, porsche , ect..ect.<P>The guy with no doubt the best handling Fiero in north America Sold it , because it was hard to be competitive. He was scary fast..but not fast enough. Were not talking a set of lowering springs and Fiero store bars.<P>Enjoy your cars , don't drive them to beat everyone on the road and fix some self Esteem problem , or prove something.<P><BR>Ok I'm done... Flame Suit on , but I hope I don't need it. It's just the truth. If I want a vette , i can go get in one , it's only a few feet away. I like the Fiero , it's not perfect , but it's an experience that it hard to describe , low , looks good and above all is quite a bit of fun to drive.<BR>and the possibilities are endless. <P>The best thing about Fiero's , is the tight knit family we have, and willingness to help each other. <P>wow , that was cheesy , but true. <P>Merry Xmas <P>Jonathan McCreery<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000041||Rare87GT||12-25-2001||02:03 AM||||I think some people forget that the Fiero that we are talking about is from 84-88.  The Vette's we are talking about are all the newer ones.  You can't really compare a 15 year old car with a car that is still in production.  Back in 84-88 the Fiero was on its way to passing the Vette up.  The Vette of the mid 80's and the Fiero of the mid 80's then I would say the Fiero was much better. But in response to the topic, I personally like my Fiero better than any Vette.  I think they look better and are much more unique.  Do you see very many Vette's running around compared to Fiero's?  That's why I love my Fiero, half the people that see it don't know what it is.  Fiero's look better and are funner!<P>------------------<BR>1987 GT (Maroon)*5 Speed*<BR>(Canadian Car)<BR>2.8L Mods--No Cat, K&N||||reg||1
Z||000042||FieroKyle||12-25-2001||08:56 PM||||I think JM said it best.||||reg||1
Z||000043||FieroKyle||12-25-2001||08:59 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroKyle:<BR><B>I think JM said it best.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well except for the 5.0L Mustangs out there that need their head sized down so they can fit it in their car.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000044||BlackStang95||12-25-2001||09:15 PM||||There's car owners everywhere that need their head sized down so they can get in their car.  Including fiero owners.  <P>And the mid-late 80's vettes were still pulling 0-60 times in the mid-high 5's.  That's still way faster than the fieros of the era.  Like jm and some others said, they are different cars build for different purposes.  The vette is an exotic, a supercar, and the fiero in its stock form is far from that, no matter how much some of the crazy owners swear its ferarri-like.  <P>This is also why in present times the cobra r was still a hair slower than the vette because the vette is an exotic, and the mustang starts out as a sporty coupe with the emphasis more on transportation, and the engineers have to start from that and try to work up to the vettes level.<P><BR>And a 400 hp civic would likely have as much invested in it as the sticker price on the vette.  And besides the performance...theyd still have a civic.  Blah.<p>[This message has been edited by BlackStang95 (edited 12-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000045||Slammed Fiero||12-25-2001||09:44 PM||||Every Mark has it's share of Jackasses. It's the cars with the high dollar price tags that seem to attract more.<P>I don't think the vette is an exotic , it's a great car in it's own right , I love our 87 , fast , fun , great all around track autocross car.  The motor is basic when it comes to technology , but the means justify the end , 155mph in 1987 stock with a single cam iron block aluminum head SBC. Easy and cheap to go fast. <P><BR>Dumba$$ mentioned that $15-20 Grand could do a lot more to a Fiero, than a vette. Perhaps... But I look at it this way. I have driven 88's with complete tubular setups , I have driven hopped up 84-87's and I drive probably one of the best handling early chassis cars around. Our vette has around the same amount of money into it's suspension and it handles a lot better. With Koni's , Big bars , Poly ect. It was born with a big advantage , it's not an Imbred (sp?) (ohh flame suit on)<P>It comes down to the family tree. The Fiero's Family tree started with cars that all ready had problems , so inorder to get them on the same level as other cars we have to do a lot of work. There are only a few people on this forum that are really into the handling geometry of the Fiero , less than 3 probably. The rest of us including me are followers , doing what were told works best. We don't have the driving forces marks like Vettes have in the form of aftermarket support. I know many of you will talk about the aftermarket suspension you bought with tubular arms ..ect..ect. A certian forum member had them on his car and ripped them off because he said "it was Junk" Looked pretty though. He won't waste his time with this thread , but he had a better handling fiero than any of us could dream of.<P>do you know why there was no vette in 83? they were designing the car still.<P>we could debate this all day and night. Someone will show up saying I put a XYZ motor in my car and I kill vettes all day long... Fortunatley for his ego he has never come across a vette driven by someone who has also taken the time and money to develop his car , and I'm sorry to say he has probably developed it to a much higher level than you.<P>Like I said , I love my Fiero to death , I know what it is ,and what it's not. It's a fun sleeper thats unique, cheap to swap motors into and brings me quite a bit of joy. I like driving it , Autocrossing it Showing it. I love the people involved with fiero's, and going to shows. <P>If I was hell bent on beating everything under the sun on the street , track and autocross course , I wouldn't have started with a Fiero.<P>There is ALWAYS a faster better prepped car than yours. A fiero suits me , thats the reason I have it. Right now Ilook foreward to dumping more money into it that I will never get out and making it reflect my own persoanl tastes.<P>Jonathan McCreery||||reg||1
Z||000046||artherd||12-26-2001||12:46 AM||||Ever driven a Ferrari?  <P>Ever driven a good Fiero?<P>Do you have *any* idea what I am talking about?<P>If it's still 'no' by the time you get to the third question, then You Just Don't Get It.<P>It's quite comprable to a 308 Ferrari actually. <P><BR>------------------------------<P>JM:<P>1991 ZR1: 3,520<P>1991 C4 C4 Coupe: 3,288 lbs.<P>the ZR1 is 232lbs heavier. That's a passanger.  Extra weight is not surprising, as it's 3" longer (and I belive 1" wider.)<P>The T6-6 is also a bugger tranny.<P>It did not feel ungainly, or like a 'autoban only' car at all in my hands.  It's stupid-fast, you just cannot drive it like a normal vette (drive around town in 4th turning 1200rpm.)<P>You've actually got to *drive* the car.<P>Redline the thing.  New concept to many of us here. (with 2.8s and iron pukes that like to explode at 5grand.)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BlackStang95:<BR><B>Dude, the fiero has more in common with the cavalier than a ferrari.  I like them too but let's not kid ourselves.  It's a 2-4000 dollar sports coupe that some guys with complexes think is a 40k italian exotic.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">     <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red                     "Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                                        -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"<p>[This message has been edited by artherd (edited 12-26-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000047||Slammed Fiero||12-26-2001||01:55 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>Ever driven a Ferrari?  <P>Ever driven a good Fiero?<P>Do you have *any* idea what I am talking about?<P>If it's still 'no' by the time you get to the third question, then You Just Don't Get It.<P>It's quite comprable to a 308 Ferrari actually. <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ewww...From my standpoint a well setup 88 is better than a 308. I had a few laps in a 77 carb'd car , Felt like a pig kind of. The fiero was more comfortable to push hard. As for the Zr1 , Once again I had a few laps in one at Powell Motorsports school (corvette driving school) even the instructors liked the Std C4 better, and were able to turn faster lap times. Now I'm sure that would differ on a track that is less technical (long straights) but I just felt more at home pushing the C4. Don't worry , I drove the Zr1 like a rental car.<P>I'm always amazed at the people who have Fiero's and work on them. Ingenious bunch of people , Lots of engineers it seems , lots of great ideas.<P>Ok , I think we should kill this thread , it's getting a wee bit off track.<P>JM <p>[This message has been edited by Slammed Fiero (edited 12-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000048||85SE||12-26-2001||11:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>Ok , I think we should kill this thread , it's getting a wee bit off track.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Uh, here comes Andrew to the rescue  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Trevor - I was wondering what your plans were for the 'vette and Fiero.  I mean, you were saying that you figured a hopped up '88 GT with 3.4 could end up running you as much as a C4 'vette.  Now, since I know you're still in school I'm going to assume you don't have the budget for a 92+ 'vette, and were instead looking at the mid 80s 'vettes.  If I'm wrong - sorry, but I'm thinking rationally here.  Though, I'd love a '96 Grand Sport myself  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Anyhow, have you considered what, say, 8000 to 10 000 Canadian dollars (what I figure may well be your budget on this car) buys you in a 'vette?  I've watched sites like <A HREF="http://www.autotrader.ca" TARGET=_blank>http://www.autotrader.ca</A>  and the only vettes that show up there in that sort of price range tend to have lots of miles on them, probably meaning (to me anyhow) that they're getting long in the tooth and are going to need engine rebuilds and or suspension/brake work soon.  Just my thinking.  I guess my point is that you may end up dropping your money on a vette, sure, but you may end up having to drop more money than you intended to spend on repairs.  That is to say, you may be able to build an '88GT with 3.4 with suspension rework and brake replacement for the same amount of money as the price of admission to a worn out vette.<P>Worth thinking about.  By the way, could you let us all know if you're still reading this thread?  Thanks!<P>Cheers,<BR>Andrew - 85SE<P>------------------<BR>Andrew Menchions - 85SE - AKA Fieroless in Newfoundland||||reg||1
Z||000049||Smoooooth GT||12-26-2001||11:55 PM||||OMG... When can we stop this CHILDISH ARGUING over this ..... ???? ||||reg||1
Z||000050||artherd||12-27-2001||07:09 AM||||I've only driven 1980s  2v and 4v injected (supposedly the early carb cars are actually faster than the injected 2v s though!) But I thought they actually felt similar to my car.  Twitchy in the rear at the limit though.  More power, though the injected 2v felt very very 'fiero'  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>The ZR1 does well on longer/faster courses I suppose. (just an all out ball to drive, I thought.  What a motor!)<P>Back on topic a tad;  Consider that if we're talking the upper range of Fieros, and the lower range of Corvettes,  then you might be better served by the low mileage 88 Fiero with a new engine, versus the beat-up older C4 that you can't afford to fix.<P>But if one car just excites you more... then you just need to get that one by golly!<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>inje <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>  Ewww...From my standpoint a well setup 88 is better than a 308. I had a few laps in a 77 carb'd car , Felt like a pig kind of. The fiero was more comfortable to push hard. As for the Zr1 , Once again I had a few laps in one at Powell Motorsports school (corvette driving school) even the instructors liked the Std C4 better, and were able to turn faster lap times. Now I'm sure that would differ on a track that is less technical (long straights) but I just felt more at home pushing the C4. Don't worry , I drove the Zr1 like a rental car.<P>I'm always amazed at the people who have Fiero's and work on them. Ingenious bunch of people , Lots of engineers it seems , lots of great ideas.<P>Ok , I think we should kill this thread , it's getting a wee bit off track.<P>JM <P>[This message has been edited by Slammed Fiero (edited 12-26-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">      <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red.                     "Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver, In-Progreess.                                        -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000051||NEPTUNE||12-27-2001||08:48 AM||||Unlike C4 Corvettes and Cadillacs, every Fiero I have ever seen came equipped with turn signals. (blinking lights used to indicate turns and lane changes)<BR>This alone makes the Fiero the obvious choice. <BR>Also no white shoes and belt are required to drive one.<P>C5 corvette- WOW! if you can afford it......<p>[This message has been edited by NEPTUNE (edited 12-27-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000052||rogergarrison||12-27-2001||02:54 PM||||Well Corvette is def not an exotic car by a long shot. Pricewise, as new cars go, its in the upper midrange, with Lincoln, Caddy, Expedition, Mercedes and BMW sedans. Handleing is not very impressive to me (talking C4). If i hit some rough road or a bump in a turn, the car switches lanes. The Ferraro stays stuck pretty good in fast corners, very like most mid engine Ferraris. Ive driven friends 308s, Testa Rossa, F40. Also traded another for his Pantera to see what we though of eachs car. It was very quick accelerating, pretty fair fast cornering. He was pretty impressed with mine especially knowing it was a converted Fiero. The best handling car Ive owned (or been lucky to test out) was the AMG SL. Front engine, rwd. That car was as stable as a rock at any speed, cornered like a slot car. You could be cruising down freeway talking to someone and not paying attention find yourself going 130 without even realizing it. Im having fun playing around town in the Vette, but when spring comes the Ferraro will be the personal choice for fun cruisin on the open road. The vette will be stored for summer  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000053||LoW_KeY||12-27-2001||04:39 PM||||Whoa man I seriously envy you how in the world did you get to drive an F-40? those are some seriously fast cars! <P>I think about the only other car I'd want about now is a Ferrari.<P>On this topic.. if you want a vette go buy one want a fiero go buy one choice is yours, I could have gotten an 84-87 vette I mean they are rather cheap.  I wasn't to impressed with how they went for having a V8 but hey those were the early years, I'd take a Z-06 anytime too  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> but I'm only 20 so I won't be buying one of those anytime soon.<P>Drive both see what you like and nab her.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: FierFormula88   <P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/low_keysig.gif"> ||||reg||1
